UNIT difficulty_filter
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
FIDELITY spec
ANCHOR the extra data Super Mario Maker sends with a 100 Mario or Course World course search, measured from a real game 2026-11: Easy ["1","0","34","","0"], Normal ["1","0","74","","0"]; Course World's bands are 0-34, 35-74, 75-95 and 96-100
SIG difficulty_filter(extra_data: list[text]) -> tuple[record[minPercent: i64, maxPercent: i64, minAttempts: i64, unrated: bool], bool]
ENVELOPE extra_data is any list of text, the empty list included.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Reads the failure-rate range a game asks for. The bool says whether a range was asked for; when it is false, any difficulty will do.
BEHAVIOR
R1 A range is asked for only when extra_data has at least 3 items and the first is exactly "1". CITED
R2 The second and third items are the lowest and highest failure rate allowed, in percent, both included. Each is read as a whole number in base 10, with an optional leading "+" or "-"; anything else (empty text, spaces, a decimal point, letters) means no range.
R3 No range either when the lowest is below 0, the highest is above 100, or the lowest is above the highest.
R4 With a range, the answer is (minPercent = the lowest, maxPercent = the highest, minAttempts = MIN_ATTEMPTS, unrated = false) and true. Items after the third are not read.
R5 With no range, the answer is (minPercent = 0, maxPercent = 0, minAttempts = 0, unrated = false) and false.
PINNED const MIN_ATTEMPTS = 10 CITED assumption the server's owner 2026-11-17: a course cleared on its only two attempts should not count as the easiest there is
